<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch07_P03_Where_Does_It_Start</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>For which positive integers \(n\) is the inequality \(n! > 2^n\) false? Enter them as a set, for example <code>{1, 2}</code>.</p>
<div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
<li><p>The induction step in the example used \(k \geq 4\), but it only needs \(k + 1 \geq 2\): if \(k! > 2^k\), then</p>
<p>\[(k+1)! = (k+1) \times k! > (k+1) \times 2^k \geq 2 \times 2^k = 2^{k+1}.\]</p>
<p>So the step works for every \(k \geq 1\). Which statement explains why the proof shows \(n! > 2^n\) only for \(n \geq 4\)?</p>
<div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Compare \(n!\) and \(2^n\) for the first few values of \(n\):</p>
<table>
<tr><td>\(n\)</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>5</td></tr>
<tr><td>\(n!\)</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>6</td><td>24</td><td>120</td></tr>
<tr><td>\(2^n\)</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>8</td><td>16</td><td>32</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The inequality is false for \(n = 1, 2, 3\) and true for \(n = 4\). The example proves it for every \(n \geq 4\), so the answer is \(\{1, 2, 3\}\).</p></li>
<li><p>The induction step needs only \(k + 1 \geq 2\), so it passes the truth of the statement from \(k\) to \(k+1\) for every \(k \geq 1\). But the statement is false for \(n = 1, 2\) and \(3\), so there is no true case to pass on until \(n = 4\). The base case must be a true statement, and the chain of implications starts from it: the first true case is \(n = 4\). An induction step on its own proves nothing.</p></li>
</ol>]]></text></generalfeedback>
